WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.689
Leon: Welcome everybody.

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:11.269
Before we proceed, I would like to thank three
people who are very instrumental in arranging

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:12.670
this event.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:17.430
Katie Earl, Lindsay Wise, and Wesley Fox.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.210
Thank you very much, guys, for all your help.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.270
But mostly, thanks to our guest Vladimir Kara-Murza.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:31.720
Well, let me start with congratulations, Volodya, on being the very first

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:37.079
recipient of the Sergei Magnitsky Human Rights
prize, I believe in London a couple of weeks

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:38.079
ago.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.079
What was it like?

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:40.989
Vladimir: Well, first of all, thank you so
much for the invitation.

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:44.539
It's always an honor and a pleasure to be
here at the American Enterprise Institute,

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:46.019
and to do events with Leon.

00:00:46.019 --> 00:00:48.579
It's becoming a tradition now.

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:55.179
And so, it was indeed on the 16th of November,
the day that Sergei Magnitsky was killed in

00:00:55.179 --> 00:01:00.199
Matrosskaya Tishina prison back in 2009, and
they had the annual Magnitsky Human Rights

00:01:00.199 --> 00:01:02.399
awards in London.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:08.690
This is a bi-annual ceremony, and I was indeed
honored and very humbled to receive one this

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:09.690
year.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.530
I don't suddenly think that I'm worthy of
any kind of award but I know I will cherish

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.050
this for the rest of my life.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:21.000
It's a great honor but more than that, I'm
very proud to have played a small part in

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.990
this process of convincing members of the
U.S. Congress, five years ago now...in fact,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:34.920
exactly five years in December 2012 this happened,
to pass a law which was named in memory and

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.370
in honor of Sergei Magnitsky, and which introduced
what should really be a simple and straightforward

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.330
principle but was, in fact, a groundbreaking
principle in international practice.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:53.280
It was the principle that responsibility for
human rights abuse should be assigned to where

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.590
it actually belongs.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.680
Not into an entire country and to all the
citizens of that country as general sanctions

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.220
do, but to the actual people who perpetrate
human rights abuses and corruption.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:09.110
So what the Magnitsky Act did is it laid down
the principle that those people, those Russian

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:13.439
officials and those oligarchs in and around
the Putin regime, who are responsible for

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:19.640
human rights abuse and corruption will no
longer be able to receive visas to the U.S.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.219
and use the U.S. financial and banking system
for their personal gain.

00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:29.840
And the reason this is so important is because
of the nature of the Putin regime, the way

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.850
it is structured, and the fundamental hypocrisy
and a double standard that is right at the

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.569
heart of the Putin regime.

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:42.709
And that is that the people who are in charge
of the Russian government today, they want

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:46.249
to steal in Russia but spend in the west.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.520
And this is the way they have done things
for years.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:54.909
The same people who undermine and violate
and abuse the most basic norms of democratic

00:02:54.909 --> 00:03:02.810
society at home want to use the privileges
and opportunities of democratic society in

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.510
Western countries for themselves and for their
families.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.370
You know, they want to educate their children
in Western schools, they want to keep their

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.370
money in western banks.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.489
When I say their money, that's the money they've
actually stolen from the Russian people.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:17.450
They want to buy high-class real estate and
yachts and luxury cars and whatever else in

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.450
western countries.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:22.810
And we think that this hypocrisy and this
double standard has to stop, and this was

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.310
the premise behind the Magnitsky Act.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:28.870
And I'm proud to have played my small part
in helping pass this measure into law.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:34.389
And when the Magnitsky Act was passed, exactly
five years ago, at the end of 2012, Boris

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:38.760
Nemtsov and I was sitting on that day in the
visitor's gallery of the U.S. House of Representatives

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:44.029
chamber watching as they were voting on this
bill and as this bill was becoming law in

00:03:44.029 --> 00:03:45.650
front of our eyes.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:52.659
And I remember Boris said on that day that,
"This is the most pro-Russian law ever passed

00:03:52.659 --> 00:03:57.540
in any foreign country because it holds to
account those who violate the rights of Russian

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.829
citizens and those who steal the money of
Russian taxpayers."

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:05.650
And I'm happy to say that since then, since
the Magnitsky Act was passed here in the U.S.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:11.629
five years ago, three more countries have
followed this example: Canada, Estonia, and

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:12.629
Lithuania.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:15.859
Lithuania just last week, the president of
Lithuania, Dalia Grybauskaitė just signed

00:04:15.859 --> 00:04:16.859
this bill into law.

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:20.769
And I'll be in Vilnus next week to meet with
members of Lithuanian parliament, and to thank

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:22.400
them for doing this.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.190
And we hope that other countries, other western
democracies, also take a lead on this issue

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:34.790
and send a message to those crooks and those
human rights abusers in the Putin regime that

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:38.290
they will no longer will be welcome in western
democratic countries.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:42.940
And there's one other thing that Boris Nemtsov
said back five years ago when the Magnitsky

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.880
law was being passed here.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:52.800
He said that when Russia becomes a democratic
country with the rule of law, he wanted to

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:58.430
be the first one to go back to the U.S. and
other western countries and advocate for a

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:00.870
repeal of the Magnitsky Act.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:05.159
Because it will no longer be needed, because
we will have a real justice system and a real

00:05:05.159 --> 00:05:10.080
rule of law system that will be able to take
care of the crooks and the abusers and the

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.080
corrupt officials.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.460
Now of course we know that he will not be
doing that.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.810
And, you know, the last two years have taught
me to be very careful about making predictions

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:24.479
but I certainly help to be able to one day
go back here and to Ottawa and to Vilnus and

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:29.930
Talin and hopefully London and other capitals
of democratic countries to say that this law

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.060
is no longer needed because we have a system
of government, a real system of government

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.500
and justice and the rule of law that is able
to protect those principles.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.620
Leon: Well, thank you very much, Valudia.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:44.430
You know, it's always...I mean, there are
multiple pleasures in chatting with you in

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:50.639
public, but one of them is that you're such
an unusual combination of, you know, of an

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:51.639
Oxford educated.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:52.639
Vladimir: Cambridge.

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:53.639
Leon: Cambridge, sorry.

00:05:53.639 --> 00:05:54.940
Vladimir: Never confuse the two.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.300
Leon: Right.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:57.650
I thought I'll test it.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.030
Vladimir: It's like if you say that I'm from
Saint Petersburg.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.870
Leon: Yeah.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:08.380
All right, Cambridge-educated historian and
opposition activist.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:14.379
So let me...you know, I mean it's the same
half, they're just maybe different halves

00:06:14.379 --> 00:06:15.820
or different colors.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:21.910
But...all right, let's stress the analyst
part of your hat.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.789
Looks like Russia is emerging from a recession.

00:06:24.789 --> 00:06:30.860
I mean, there are various reasons why, primarily
it's not because of investment.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:37.069
It's primarily because people borrow and spend.

00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:44.970
And...but whatever the reason, the consensus
is about 1.7% GDP growth.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.490
At the same time, paradoxically, this is the
fourth year in a row where the real incomes

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.939
are falling.

00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:58.629
And Alexei Kudrin, to whom we'll return--probably
known to everybody, I don't need to introduce

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:04.180
him here--said that poverty is a real problem
in Russia.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:12.300
And the so-called social protests are up this
year, 56% over the last year.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:18.919
And people protest, you know, the higher...the
rising utility costs, healthcare, transportation,

00:07:18.919 --> 00:07:20.669
housing, layoffs.

00:07:20.669 --> 00:07:30.960
And I thought I would never hear it from...since
the post-revolutionary impoverished 1990s

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.509
when the oil was $18 a barrel, wage arrears.

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:41.479
I said so-called social protests because both
the regime and the opposition seem to distinguish

00:07:41.479 --> 00:07:49.379
rather sharply between what's going in those
protests and what they call political protests.

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:53.810
Where they are...they could be called political,
they're strictly localized.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.900
Or at least that seems to me.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:58.240
I mean, people protest against a particular
mayor, a particular governor, a particular

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:04.069
police force, particular municipality.

00:08:04.069 --> 00:08:11.139
But we have not seen the repeat of the March
26th National Anti-Corruption Demonstration

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:15.650
in, I believe, 84 Russian cities.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:20.300
Now, the...I wonder though.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:31.760
In an economy where 70% of the GDP is either
owned or controlled by the government, or

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:37.870
a subsidized by the government in the public
sector, there's always a tinge of politics

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:41.060
in every social protest, is that right?

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:42.320
Vladimir: Well, absolutely.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:46.589
There's always a fine line between social
and political protest, and it's not always

00:08:46.589 --> 00:08:47.589
clear where you draw it.

00:08:47.589 --> 00:08:52.430
I mean, for example, a couple of years ago
we had those protest by long haul truck drivers

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.720
around the country against this new road tarrif
system that was introduced.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:59.010
And on the one hand you can say that, well,
that's a social protest because they're just

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.620
protesting against those new tarrifs that
they have to pay.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:06.190
But of course, we know that that entire system
was introduced for the personal gain of the

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:10.930
Rotenberg Brothers who are, you know, one
of the closest oligarchs, politically and

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.520
financially, to Putin and his regime.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.550
So, I mean it's debatable whether that was
social or political.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:21.550
But I think what's interesting about it, we
always had this notion for years in Russia,

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:25.920
maybe for decades, and you've written a lot
about it too that, you know, at some point,

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.589
it's those economic and social protests that
could become the drivers of political change.

00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:36.770
But I think actually if you look at the, you
know, the last 18 years now already of the

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:44.610
Putin regime, the biggest political, the biggest
protest, the biggest street protest that happened

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:48.810
under the Putin regime happened actually when
the economy was doing very well in December

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.010
2011-January 2012.

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:54.070
It had in fact nothing to do with social or
economic grievances.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:57.630
In fact, the people who came out on Bolotnaya and Sakharov Avenue back

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:02.820
in December 2011, six years ago now, they
didn't come for money or for wages or for

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.269
any social and economic demands.

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:06.260
They came for their dignity.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:10.920
They came because they felt insulted at such
a blatant stealing of their votes.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.890
You know, "We Are Not Cattle" was one of the
most popular slogans on Bolotnaya

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.320
on December the 10th, 2011.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.730
And in fact, a lot of the people who came
out to protest were representative of the

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:24.010
relatively affluent urban middle classes.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:27.160
And again, it was not about the social or
the economic issues.

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:28.160
It was about dignity.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.820
It was about people wanting to be citizens
in their own country, as opposed to voiceless

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.940
subjects who could be told, you know, that
I'll be president, he'll be prime minister

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.360
for the next 12 years, goodbye.

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.880
You know, that was the driving issue.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:45.790
And of course, those protests that you just
referred to that we saw all across the country

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:50.699
in the last few months, in March and then
again in June and then again in the last few

00:10:50.699 --> 00:10:56.740
weeks with the so-called presidential election
campaign beginning and going underway, of

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:00.910
course at the March protest, as you pointed
out exactly took place in more than 80 towns

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.010
and cities across Russia.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.350
In June, it was already almost 200.

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:07.230
It was a much bigger wave on June the 12th,
Russian national day.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:11.209
Symbolically the day when the Russian
parliament back in 1990 doctored the declaration

00:11:11.209 --> 00:11:14.770
of sovereignty from the Soviet central government.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:20.029
And I think the most striking feature of those
protests...I mean obstentiably, they were

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:24.480
about corruption and against corruption, the
pervasive government corruption, the immediate

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:30.050
trigger was that investigation done by Navalny's
anti-corruption foundation into the secret

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.940
financial empire of current prime minister,
Dmitry Medvedev.

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:37.649
You know, his yachts, his villas, his vineyards
in Tuscany, and all the other stuff.

00:11:37.649 --> 00:11:40.550
But it was, of course, about much more than
that.

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:45.370
He was not just against the corruption itself,
it was also against the reasons, the underlying

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:49.380
reasons that allow for this corruption, the
lack of transparency, the lack of accountability,

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.800
the lack of the rule of law.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:58.149
And at the end of the day, the fact that one
person has now been in power in our country

00:11:58.149 --> 00:12:01.449
for 18 years, let's just pause and think about
it.

00:12:01.449 --> 00:12:03.970
Putin has now, if you count by the number
of days, Putin has now been in power longer

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:08.079
than Leonid Brezhnev, the symbol of Soviet
totallitarian stagnation.

00:12:08.079 --> 00:12:12.440
You know, the generation that...the generation
of our parents grew up knowing nobody but

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:13.440
Brezhnev will now [crosstalk 00:12:12].

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.120
Leon: Careful there on the generation of our
parents.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:19.410
Vladimir: Well, it's back for Oxford, Leon.

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:25.870
We now have an entire generation in Russia
who grew up not knowing anything except the

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:26.910
Putin regime.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:30.430
When I go around the country to different
regions as part of my work with the Open Russian

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:34.290
Movement and speak to this young activists
in different places, you know, someone who's

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:39.310
18, 20, 22, I have to remind myself that I'm
speaking to someone who does not know anything

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:40.310
except Putin.

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:44.589
You know, I'm old enough to remember what
real elections are like in Russia.

00:12:44.589 --> 00:12:48.209
I'm old enough to remember the government
and the president harshly criticized on national

00:12:48.209 --> 00:12:49.560
TV.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.660
The people who are now coming out to protest
all across the country, for them it's something

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:55.970
out, you know, out of fantasy books.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:57.649
They don't know this.

00:12:57.649 --> 00:13:03.530
But they instinctively understand increasingly
that it's not okay, in a European country,

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:09.389
in the 21st century, to have 1 man staying
in power for 18 years without regards for

00:13:09.389 --> 00:13:12.360
such nice things as free elections and term
limits.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:16.091
And they increasingly understand that the
fact that the government is unchangeable,

00:13:16.091 --> 00:13:20.110
and that it's not possible to change it, that
elections are not real, that there are no

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:25.070
checks and balances, there are no real institutions,
that all that is the cause of the pervasive

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:30.100
corruption of the abuses of basic principles
of rule of law and human rights.

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:36.139
And there is this growing awareness of the
link between the socio-economic problems that

00:13:36.139 --> 00:13:38.420
exist, and the political situation that have
caused them.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:44.750
And I think also, and this is what I just
saw from, you know, again, going all across

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.819
Russia in the last three years and speaking
to these people, to these activists in different

00:13:48.819 --> 00:13:57.149
regions, we are seeing increasingly with many
of these young people their sense of dignity

00:13:57.149 --> 00:14:02.120
and their self awareness as citizens of their
country gradually becoming stronger than the

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.889
sense of fear of political repression.

00:14:04.889 --> 00:14:06.270
I think that is a very important turning point.

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:10.130
It's not gonna happen overnight, it's not
gonna be a momentous event.

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:13.700
But it's gonna be...it's a gradual process
but it's already happening.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:16.970
And I think we saw this very clearly with
the protest waves in March and in June.

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:20.750
Many of the people who came out to protest
in March, of course this was the first time

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.829
ever that they came out to take part in a
political protest.

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:28.000
And many of them, as you know, more than a
thousand people on a single day were arrested.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:33.410
Many of them were jailed, given 10, 5, 10,
15 days of administrative jail terms for,

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:34.899
quote-unquote, mass disturbances.

00:14:34.899 --> 00:14:39.399
This is what, this is what Kremlin speak for
taking part of peaceful demonstrations.

00:14:39.399 --> 00:14:44.750
And when they were coming out from those jails
after those administrative terms, you know,

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:48.160
they'll be approached by journalist or by,
you know, just friends and supporters and

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:52.930
asked, "Well, presumably now, you're not gonna
go to protest again after this?"

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.560
They said, "No, no, no, we're definitely coming
out again after this."

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:59.670
And in June, there were more people in more
places, more cities and towns participating

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:00.940
in the protest than in March.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:09.410
So I think this a very hopeful trend for Russia,
but a very worrying one for the Putin regime.

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:15.649
Leon: Volodya, to watch
Russian TV, to certainly to listen to the

00:15:15.649 --> 00:15:22.600
Duma deputies, and to see some of the most
popular talk shows, you would think that Russia

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:27.980
is at a state of war, and Vladimir Putin is
a wartime president.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:37.560
Just the other week, he held a marathon four-day,
nonstop session with the top Russian military

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:39.510
commanders.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:47.779
And as part of finalizing yet another state
armament program, this one is over $300 billion

00:15:47.779 --> 00:15:53.720
continuation from the first program inaugurated
in 2008, in much higher rubles--at that time

00:15:53.720 --> 00:16:01.040
it was $700 billion, but by now it's probably
the same--bottom line is Russia is committed,

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:11.450
this one is to run to 2027, in the past...less
than 20 years, spent over 2/3 of a trillion

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:16.200
dollars, one armament.

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:22.630
At the signing of the program, according to
the Russian media, Putin directed all major

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:28.319
enterprises, irrespective of ownership, to
be prepared to expend defense-related production,

00:16:28.319 --> 00:16:31.490
as he put it, in the time of need.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:35.110
I can't recall anything like this from before
peristroika.

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:36.110
What is going on here?

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:40.139
Vladimir: Yeah, he also said that every type
of production Russia must be prepared to switch

00:16:40.139 --> 00:16:43.860
to voyennoye [foreign language 00:16:42], military reign
of course.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:47.660
All these jokes on social media about what's
the right caliber of the pasta that will need

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:48.660
to be produced.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:50.129
But you're right.

00:16:50.129 --> 00:16:54.699
We have...I mean, we heard it for a long time
but we had heard that before.

00:16:54.699 --> 00:17:00.181
And if you look at Russian state TV, you know,
so called talk shows--you know, the hour of

00:17:00.181 --> 00:17:04.180
hate, again, to use the old Soviet term--if
you watch...

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:05.700
Leon: It's actually an Orwellian term, actually.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:06.700
Vladimir: Yes.

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:08.069
But we use that a lot, of course.

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:12.209
So if you watch those--which I advice you,
by the way, not to do if you value your nervous

00:17:12.209 --> 00:17:16.439
system but I sometimes have to just to know
what they're thinking and what they doing--you

00:17:16.439 --> 00:17:21.579
would hear this constant...well, warmongering
is probably the best way to describe it.

00:17:21.579 --> 00:17:29.100
And the propaganda, and just the intensification
of hate, and of to turning the U.S. into radioactive

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:31.850
ash and talk about the west now as enemies.

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:36.169
And of course, this constant search for traitors
and national traitors and fifth columnists

00:17:36.169 --> 00:17:39.450
and foreign agents inside of Russia.

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:44.110
And this has been going on for years now at
an increasing phase because...well frankly,

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:48.370
that's the only thing this regime has to stand
on.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:54.159
They have to kind of ratchet up this rhetoric
because they have not much to, you know, to

00:17:54.159 --> 00:17:59.030
point to in terms of their economic success
because there is none or there are very few.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:04.690
You know, they like to pretend this, of course,
as you know, this catchphrase that Putin propaganda

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:11.559
often uses to describe what his regime has
supposedly done, is that they raised Russia

00:18:11.559 --> 00:18:12.559
from its knees.

00:18:12.559 --> 00:18:14.030
That's the phrase that has been used for years.

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:18.380
But, you know, if you actually look at what
they have done in terms of, okay, let's accept

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.429
their terms and let's look at what have they
done in, what have they done in terms of foreign

00:18:21.429 --> 00:18:28.110
policy, and geopolitics, you know, under president
Boris Yeltsin, who is now a demonized figure

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:33.679
in Putin state TV, Russia became a member
of the G8, for example, the most respected

00:18:33.679 --> 00:18:35.460
club of world powers.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.809
Under Mr. Putin, we were expelled from the
G8.

00:18:37.809 --> 00:18:41.510
I mean, what's more...where is that rising
from the knees?

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:48.250
Because of the Putin regime's behavior, we
now have very harsh, very biting general economic

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:52.820
sanctions introduced against our country by
the leading economic powers of the world.

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:56.179
For me, that's not really something in line
with Russian national interests.

00:18:56.179 --> 00:19:02.020
So they have nothing to stand on in terms
of real achievements, so they have to build

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:06.830
up this wall of pretense and propaganda that
everybody...you know, we are a beseeched fortress,

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:07.990
everybody is against us.

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:10.520
And of course, the enemies themselves change
from year to year.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:12.080
One year it's Estonia, the other year is Georgia.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:15.679
And now, of course, it has been...that place
has been firmly taken by the United States

00:19:15.679 --> 00:19:17.159
for many years now.

00:19:17.159 --> 00:19:21.320
Because that's the only thing they have to
go on.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:26.809
I mean, we can discuss of what's actually
real in this, and I know some people are seriously

00:19:26.809 --> 00:19:29.971
raising the prospect, "Oh, does that mean
there'll be war at some point," or something

00:19:29.971 --> 00:19:36.100
like that but I think this is mostly just
a propaganda image that they need to present

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:41.600
something in the public sphere because they
have nothing real to present and to show.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:45.530
Leon: All right, we'll come back to Putin
as a wartime president.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:51.860
Oh, a footnote is that one of the products,
probably, a more conspicuous product of that

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:59.909
state arming program is what, in the NATO
designation, a beret A-class nuclear submarine,

00:19:59.909 --> 00:20:07.480
outfitted with 20 intercontinental ballistic
missile tubes, launching tubes, each capable

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:14.250
of carrying 6 independently targeted nuclear
warheads.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:17.040
And the name of that submarine is Prince Vladimir.

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:21.130
Vladimir: We have one standing by the Kremlin
now, as you know as well, on the [crosstalk

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:22.130
00:20:21].

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:23.130
Leon: No, I didn't know that.

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:26.360
Vladimir: Oh, there's a new monument put up
last year, just on the side of the Borovitsky Gate,

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:27.539
to Prince Vladimir.

00:20:27.539 --> 00:20:28.760
Leon: Yes.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:33.910
Well, the fascinating issue is also, I think,
I believe the eighth monument to Alexander

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:42.230
III, which is probably, in modern times, the
most reactionary Russian leader.

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:45.070
If we have time, we'll touch on this.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:49.510
Volodya a little bit about the state of opposition.

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:55.410
Ksenia Sobchak presumably wanted to run from
PARNAS, the party of Boris Nemtsov,

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:59.360
I believe your party at some point as well.

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:07.070
But PARNAS demurred, specifically Mikhail
Kasyanov, citing the lack of agreement with

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:10.809
Yavlinsky and Navalny.

00:21:10.809 --> 00:21:17.250
And so Sobchak is now running from a fairly
obscure party, Civic Initiative, assuming,

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:21.450
of course, she collects 100,000 signatures.

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:29.830
Her program strikes me as quite liberal, especially,
you know, concerning Crimea, Ukraine, relation

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:31.690
to...with the west.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:34.040
What is going on here?

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:35.040
Could you tell us?

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:37.380
It's a little bit complex for me.

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:42.289
Vladimir: Well, I think what's going on is
the same that has been happening in the last

00:21:42.289 --> 00:21:47.070
few election cycles, national election cycles
in Russia.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:55.669
So as you recall, beginning from at least
2008, genuine opponents of Mr. Putin have

00:21:55.669 --> 00:21:59.150
been blocked from participating elections,
have been blocked from the ballot.

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:04.740
In 2008, this was Mikhail Kasyanov the former
prime minister and Vladimir Bukovsky, the

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.100
famous Soviet-era dissident.

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:12.200
In 2012, at the height of the protests, it
was Grigory Yavlinsky who was blocked from

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:13.200
the ballot.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:17.900
And according to serious analysis from the
time, he could've got quite a lot of votes

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:19.640
because that was the height of the protest
movement.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:21.610
And he's not really a polarizing figure.

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:25.080
I mean a lot of people could've supported
him just because that's the way of protesting

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:26.080
against the regime.

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:32.570
They removed him, and instead of him, back
in 2012, as you remember well, the Kremlin

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:38.360
put up the billionaire Mikhail Prokhorov to
imitate an opposition presence on the ballot.

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:44.039
And in fact, Prokhorov actually missed the
deadline for submitting his nomination papers

00:22:44.039 --> 00:22:45.840
to the central electoral commission.

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:49.770
So the central electoral commission waited
until 3 AM for Prokhorov's representatives

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:50.770
to come in.

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:55.010
I think that's an unprecedented event in Russian
electoral practice, and it's clear why.

00:22:55.010 --> 00:23:01.179
So when you say that... when you say diplomatically
that provided Ksenia Sobchak collects the

00:23:01.179 --> 00:23:04.860
required signatures, I don't think that's
any kind of a problem.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:11.900
There were two genuine opposition leaders
who were planning to challenge Vladimir Putin

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:15.289
in the 2018 presidential election.

00:23:15.289 --> 00:23:20.179
One was Boris Nemtsov, and he will not be
challenging Putin because in February of 2012,

00:23:20.179 --> 00:23:22.900
he was killed on the bridge in front of the
Kremlin.

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:27.830
And the second is Alexei Navalny, who unless
something astonishing happens--which I don't

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:33.730
think it will--will not be on the ballot in
March because he was deliberately disenfranchised

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:38.000
by the Russian authorities through a politically
motivated cooked up court conviction.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:41.880
Which was, by the way, overturned earlier
this year by the European Court of Human Rights

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:46.950
but was then repeated, word for word, comma
for comma, in a repeat conviction which was

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:51.440
exactly the same as the one overturned by
the ECHR earlier this year.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:57.870
So in the absence of genuine opponents on
the ballot, I think the Kremlin still cares

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:03.250
about the international legitimacy, or at
least the veneer of international legitimacy

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:04.860
of the so-called electoral process.

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:08.320
And we can talk about more of this later because
I think that's a very important point, the

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:10.760
international aspect of this.

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:16.980
So I think what they're doing with Ksenia
Sobchak is trying to create that veneer of

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:20.720
international legitimacy, that look, we have
an opponent on the ballot.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:25.240
Of course, she's someone who's been, well
I think we can say a family friend of Vladimir

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:26.240
Putin for years.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:30.549
She's the daughter of his former boss, former
St. Petersburg mayor Natoly Sobchak, who brought

00:24:30.549 --> 00:24:35.669
Putin into politics, who brought him basically
where he is today.

00:24:35.669 --> 00:24:42.409
And she said himself several times that she's
very grateful to Mr. Putin personally for

00:24:42.409 --> 00:24:47.820
what she considers saving her father's life
back in 1997 when there was a prosecution

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:53.020
being prepared against him, and Putin took
him outside of Russia to Paris in late '90s,

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:54.020
if you recall.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:25:00.820
So I think...let's not talk about Ksenia Sobchak
as a serious opposition contender.

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:07.140
I think this is all a part of the Kremlin's
effort to build up a facade of a legitimate

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:08.140
electoral process.

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:14.010
Whereas in fact, as everybody knows well,
we have not had a real competitive democratic

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.800
election in Russia for years.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:17.649
And this is not me who is saying this.

00:25:17.649 --> 00:25:24.420
If you look at every single report by observers
from the OSCE and the council of Europe, in

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:29.159
every national election in Russia after the
year 2000, they have concluded that every

00:25:29.159 --> 00:25:31.909
one of them failed to meet basic democratic
standards.

00:25:31.909 --> 00:25:34.860
It was not free, not fair, and not competitive.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:40.710
I do not think we can expect any surprises
in this regard on March the 18th.

00:25:40.710 --> 00:25:44.310
And since we are on this point, I do want
to talk about the international aspect of

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:45.310
this.

00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:50.460
Since we are meeting in Washington, the capital
of our fellow member state in the OSCE, the

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:55.380
Organization for Security and Cooperation
in Europe, one of the main mandates of which

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:57.659
is actually election observation.

00:25:57.659 --> 00:26:03.990
So every time, after the year 2000, observers
from OSCE and also from the Council of Europe

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:09.090
assessed that elections in Russia do not meet
the basic standards of democracy.

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:12.409
They are not free, not fair, and not democratic.

00:26:12.409 --> 00:26:17.800
Yet every single time, the leaders of those
saying countries, of the OSCE and the Council

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:22.030
of Europe, the leaders of western democracies,
recognized the results of those so-called

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:25.169
elections and called Putin to congratulate.

00:26:25.169 --> 00:26:30.440
Now, I'm puzzled as to how those two things
make sense at the same time.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:34.760
I'll never forget March the 5th of 2012.

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:37.700
This was the day after Putin's so-called election
victory in 2012.

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:42.390
We had a big opposition rally on Pushkinskaya
Square, right in downtown Moscow, a couple

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:43.890
of miles down from the Kremlin.

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:48.770
And I remember there were tens of thousands
of people, and we're standing on a stage,

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:50.809
all the main opposition leaders in Russia
were on that stage.

00:26:50.809 --> 00:26:53.850
Boris Nemtsov was there, Grigory Yavlinsky
was there, Alexei Navalny was there, Garry

00:26:53.850 --> 00:26:54.850
Kasparov was there.

00:26:54.850 --> 00:26:59.900
And there were tens of thousands of people
just a sea of faces and flags.

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:05.090
People, Russian citizens who came to express
their anger at their votes being stolen in

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.429
such a blatant fashion.

00:27:07.429 --> 00:27:11.320
And as we were standing there...so there's
an eight hour difference, as you know, between

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:12.320
Moscow and Washington.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:16.770
So it was about 7 PM in Moscow, so it must
be have been 11 AM here.

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:25.870
And as we were standing there, we got news
that the U.S. administration issued a statement

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:27.320
of congratulations.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:33.820
Not only to Putin, which would've been half
of the problem, but to the people of Russia

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:35.560
for having held these presidential elections.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:39.559
And I have to tell you, this felt like an
insult.

00:27:39.559 --> 00:27:40.820
And it wasn't just the U.S.

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:46.110
That same day, on the 5th of March, David
Cameron, the then prime minister of the United

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:50.799
Kingdom became the first western leader to
call Putin and congratulate him on the election

00:27:50.799 --> 00:27:52.120
victory.

00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:58.510
And he did so on the same day, and alongside
Hugo Chavez and Bashar al-Assad.

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:04.340
So we really hope, and I know I speak on behalf
of many colleagues when I say this, we really

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:08.909
hope, first of all, that there'll be vigorous
international observation of the so-called

00:28:08.909 --> 00:28:11.840
presidential election in Russia on the 18th
of March.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:17.690
We hope that observers from our fellow OSCE
member states, including the U.S. make their

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:22.539
conclusions and hold the Kremlin authorities
to account with regard to the international

00:28:22.539 --> 00:28:26.760
obligations that the Russian federation has
undertaken in election standards, under such

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:31.080
documents as the OSCE Charter of Paris, the
OSCE Copenhagen Document, and protocol number

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:33.419
one to the European Convention of Human RIghts.

00:28:33.419 --> 00:28:38.309
And if these conclusions and these reports
are similar to the ones we have seen every

00:28:38.309 --> 00:28:44.669
time for the last 17 years, I really hope
that the leaders of western democracies do

00:28:44.669 --> 00:28:50.220
not legitimize the results of the so-called
election and do not congratulate Mr. Putin

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:52.240
on yet another successful theft.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:55.460
Because in effect, that is what they're congratulating
him on.

00:28:55.460 --> 00:29:01.440
And I think frankly, this is the least we
can expect of the leaders of countries that

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:04.970
pride themselves in having democratic governance
and the rule of law.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:10.630
Leon: Volodya, maybe I should
combine...there was a part of my question

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:17.730
essentially hinting at the perennial inability
of the Russian opposition to unite.

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:25.549
When I said that, you know, PARNAS cited inability
to...the inability to create a united front

00:29:25.549 --> 00:29:37.480
with Navalny and Yavlinsky, why don't we combine
that also with the so-called systemic opposition?

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:42.039
I promise to return to Alexei Kudrin, I'm
returning to him.

00:29:42.039 --> 00:29:51.070
He chaired the congress just the other week
of the Civic Forum, which is his presumably,

00:29:51.070 --> 00:29:56.530
you know, loyal but nevertheless opposition.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:30:03.750
And there was even a debate between him and
the mayor of Moscow, Sergei Sobyanin.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:05.309
So what is going on there?

00:30:05.309 --> 00:30:10.299
Vladimir: Well I remember, as you pointed
out right at the beginning, I'm also a historian

00:30:10.299 --> 00:30:12.090
as one of the hats.

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:19.059
And I remember reading so much about the debates
that were going on in the west among western

00:30:19.059 --> 00:30:24.820
criminologists about the so-called doves and
hawks in the Politbureau.

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:28.870
That there was supposed to be different camps
and these were really good ones.

00:30:28.870 --> 00:30:35.380
And, you know, wink-wink, they're really fighting
for something good within the system.

00:30:35.380 --> 00:30:41.350
And then in 1991-92 when Yelsin opened the
archives, especially during the process, the

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:44.250
judicial process against the Communist Party
of the Soviet Union at the constitutional

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:50.620
court in 1992 when many of the former central
committee archives were open, all these documents

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:52.190
came out.

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:56.570
And we saw that in fact, all of those so-called
doves and so-called hawks in the Politbureau

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:58.870
voted exactly the same way on all the major
issues.

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:02.000
War in Afghanistan and, you know, the exile
of Sakharov to Gorky, everything.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:03.630
They were all in complete agreement.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:05.830
So it was just all a big hoax.

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:13.580
So I wouldn't, to be honest, pay as much attention
to those different, you know, call it variations

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:15.130
within the system, within the regime.

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:20.380
Because although, you know, some of them are
supposedly more reactionary, some are supposedly

00:31:20.380 --> 00:31:25.690
more liberal, some of them are certainly much
more, you know, pleasant on the personal level.

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:27.750
I mean, Alexei Kudrin is a very nice man.

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:29.179
I see him from time to time in Moscow.

00:31:29.179 --> 00:31:32.720
It's always pleasant and interesting to have
conversations with him.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:36.410
If you compare him with somebody like Rogozin,
who is supposedly on the other spectrum of

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:37.450
this regime, there's no comparison.

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:38.909
But that's the personal level.

00:31:38.909 --> 00:31:39.909
Leon: First deputy prime minister.

00:31:39.909 --> 00:31:41.309
Vladimir: Yes, absolutely.

00:31:41.309 --> 00:31:46.320
The guy who said once that he'd gladly exchange
his current position for sitting in the trenches

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:47.539
in the Donetsk People's Republic.

00:31:47.539 --> 00:31:51.380
And of course, people have then remarked that
he probably wouldn't fit any trenches in there

00:31:51.380 --> 00:31:53.570
[crosstalk 00:31:51] if you saw what he looks
like.

00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:57.580
But again, I think we can discuss the differences
on the personal level.

00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:03.269
But I think fundamentally, all of these people,
they're all part of this regime and they all

00:32:03.269 --> 00:32:05.659
work for its preservation.

00:32:05.659 --> 00:32:11.370
And it's...maybe in a little bit changed form
but still for its preservation.

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:14.130
And you mentioned that term, of course, systemic
and non-systemic opposition.

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:16.250
I think that is a key term.

00:32:16.250 --> 00:32:20.710
You know, I would just call it...you know,
non-systemic opposition is a real opposition.

00:32:20.710 --> 00:32:22.081
The so-called systemic opposition is not.

00:32:22.081 --> 00:32:27.160
And I always compare it to how the East German
parliament--well, by the way, of course, Mr.

00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:30.360
Putin spent many years in East Germany--I
remember when he was coming to power 18 years

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:35.539
ago, people who wanted to see something good
in everything, they said, "Oh, well he spent

00:32:35.539 --> 00:32:36.539
time in Germany.

00:32:36.539 --> 00:32:37.740
He must been influenced by it."

00:32:37.740 --> 00:32:39.850
Well, he wasn't, although it was the wrong
Germany, of course.

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:40.960
That's the point.

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:45.190
And the East German parliament, as you remember,
also had a multi-party system, on the face

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:46.190
of it.

00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:47.860
It was not a one party state like the Soviet
Union.

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:50.889
They actually had the Christian Democrat Union,
they had the Liberal Democrat Party, they

00:32:50.889 --> 00:32:52.950
had a Peasant's Party, I think something else.

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:54.980
But of course, that was all a big hoax.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:58.350
Also, they all voted together with Mr. Honrecke
all the time.

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:04.320
And so it is for the current so-called [inaudible
00:33:01] party official system in the Duma

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:05.630
and what exists in our country.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:11.179
All of these so-called Duma opposition leaders
are completely unanimous when it comes to

00:33:11.179 --> 00:33:12.419
all the major points for the regime.

00:33:12.419 --> 00:33:13.630
In fact, we have...

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:15.149
I was speaking at a conference recently.

00:33:15.149 --> 00:33:21.799
There was a representatives of the opposition
from Papua and New Guinea.

00:33:21.799 --> 00:33:23.240
And he said he cannot speak.

00:33:23.240 --> 00:33:28.580
And he said, "Well, the situation in our country
is so bad, we only have one real opposition

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:29.650
member in Parliament."

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:32.820
And when the break began, I came up to him
and I said, "Well you know, you're really

00:33:32.820 --> 00:33:34.429
lucky because that's one more than we have.

00:33:34.429 --> 00:33:35.429
We have zero."

00:33:35.429 --> 00:33:39.750
There's not a single genuine opposition member
of the current Russian Duma.

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:41.940
Leon: With Gudkov expelled and...or leaving,
yeah.

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:42.940
Vladimir: Yes.

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:44.149
Well, basically expelled, yeah.

00:33:44.149 --> 00:33:47.659
Until last year, there was one, Dmitri Gudkov.

00:33:47.659 --> 00:33:52.809
And of course, again, Boris Nemtsov was planning
to run for the Russian parliament last year

00:33:52.809 --> 00:33:53.809
in 2016.

00:33:53.809 --> 00:33:54.809
In fact, as you know...

00:33:54.809 --> 00:33:55.809
Leon: From Yaroslavl.

00:33:55.809 --> 00:33:56.809
Vladimir: Absolutely.

00:33:56.809 --> 00:33:59.220
In fact, he was, again, to go back to your
term, non-systemic opposition.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:03.769
Of course, the way we use those terms, the
non-systemic opposition is supposed to signify

00:34:03.769 --> 00:34:08.460
genuine, real opposition, as opposed to the
systemic one which is the people who pretend

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:10.470
to be opposition who sit on legislative bodies.

00:34:10.470 --> 00:34:11.540
And those two things are incompatible.

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:16.010
Either you're real opposition or you sit in
a legislative body.

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:22.060
Boris Nemtsov became the first and so far
the only leader of the non-systemic opposition

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:26.720
to actually win an election as he did in Yaroslavl
four years ago in 2013.

00:34:26.720 --> 00:34:30.130
Despite all the odds, despite all the obstacles,
he won election to the regional legislature

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:31.130
there.

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:36.090
And he was planning to run for the Russian
parliament in 2016 from Yaroslavl, from the

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:37.350
single member district.

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:43.570
Now remember, when...at the late 2014, beginning
2015, his campaign was already kind of beginning

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:44.570
to be underway.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:48.250
He had a campaign staff formed, newspaper
registered, and they were taking the first

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:52.390
initial surveys, polling in the district.

00:34:52.390 --> 00:34:55.030
And I remember that this will only, they only
have time to do one.

00:34:55.030 --> 00:35:01.230
But that first initial poll showed his starting
position at 25%, which people who knew which

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:04.720
more about, you know, election campaign technologies than I do told me, it's an amazingly

00:35:04.720 --> 00:35:10.420
high number, for the start, before anything
was even done in terms of real campaigning.

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:16.370
So, there was a good chance that he was going
to win a seat in the current Russian parliament.

00:35:16.370 --> 00:35:18.490
And then of course he was preparing to challenge
Putin in 2018.

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:22.120
Leon: Well, Volodya, it's
such a rare treat to have you, well, to snatch

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:24.140
you between your various trips.

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:29.900
I'm sure a lot of people would wanna ask you
questions, so let me ask you one last one.

00:35:29.900 --> 00:35:37.610
So returning to Putin as the war-time president,
you know, war-time presidents generally are

00:35:37.610 --> 00:35:41.800
not supposed to be changed unless the war
ends, preferably in victory.

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:56.440
And there has been kind of a swirl of rumors
in the Russian media about proposed changes

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:57.800
in the constitution.

00:35:57.800 --> 00:36:06.530
Among other things, you know, nullifying the
ban on more than two consecutive terms, or

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:11.250
creating a parliamentary republic so Putin
become the prime minister, executive prime

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:17.490
minister from whatever party he would like.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:21.280
So what do you think of all of this?

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:27.570
And beyond that, probably anticipating the
question, what will happen in 2024?

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:30.180
Putin is going to be only 72.

00:36:30.180 --> 00:36:34.470
I mean, look at Mugabe.

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:39.390
He's finally out of power, but that's a very
young age.

00:36:39.390 --> 00:36:40.810
So what do you think?

00:36:40.810 --> 00:36:47.350
I've recently read a paper where the assumption
was, "Well, you know, Putin will spend his

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:55.770
term thinking of a successor because, you
know, he can't run anymore."

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:57.490
Will he be thinking of a successor?

00:36:57.490 --> 00:36:59.240
I'm not sure.

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:03.320
Vladimir: I think I would...there are two
very important questions in there.

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:08.880
I start with the one, kind of the one connected
to the regime, your last point.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:12.021
I think it was Harold Wilson, the British
prime minister, who once said a week is a

00:37:12.021 --> 00:37:14.160
long time in politics.

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:17.970
So I think it's...you know, to talk about
what's gonna happen in Russia in seven years,

00:37:17.970 --> 00:37:20.850
that's in the realm of fantasy.

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:25.670
What I would say is that it was if...first
of all, I do think Putin wants to stay in

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:26.670
power forever.

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:27.850
I do think that.

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:29.370
I think he made that decision...

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:31.280
Leon: I happen to agree.

00:37:31.280 --> 00:37:33.650
Vladimir: ...pretty early on around 2003,
which was in many ways, a turning point in

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:34.650
his regime.

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:37.420
That was the year, just in one year, three
major things happened.

00:37:37.420 --> 00:37:43.130
He shut down the last independent nationwide
television channel, TVS.

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:49.260
He jailed Khodorkovsky, which was an unmistakable
message to the Russia's business community

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:52.930
that it was best to stay out of politics,
and basically it was an attack on any kind

00:37:52.930 --> 00:37:55.680
of independent activity by the big business.

00:37:55.680 --> 00:38:01.200
And it was, of course, the year of the first
rigged national election in December 2003,

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:05.870
the election which saw genuine democratic
opposition ejected from the state Duma, both

00:38:05.870 --> 00:38:08.540
[inaudible 00:38:06] and Yabloko, both pro-democracy
liberal parties.

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:13.640
So I think it was back then that he made the
decision that he wants to stay in power forever.

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:17.170
And everything that he's done since has, I
think, corroborated that.

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:23.960
But on the other hand, if it was up to...if
it was only up to the dictators and their

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:29.760
wishes to stay in power forever, then every
dictator would stay in power forever everywhere

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:31.020
in the history of the world.

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:33.370
And of course, as we know, that's not what
happens.

00:38:33.370 --> 00:38:41.580
And Russian history has a funny way of having
major, you know, shifting massive political

00:38:41.580 --> 00:38:45.050
change start like this.

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:46.391
And this is a good year to discuss it.

00:38:46.391 --> 00:38:51.210
It's the Jubilee year in 2017.

00:38:51.210 --> 00:38:54.530
I'm old enough to remember August 1991.

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:59.150
And, you know, this was in many ways, a formative
experience for me and for people in my generation.

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:05.720
And, you know, if someone said at the beginning
of August of 1991 that by the end of August

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:08.910
1991 there would not be a Soviet regime, I
think not many people would have believed

00:39:08.910 --> 00:39:09.910
it.

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:10.910
But that is what happened.

00:39:10.910 --> 00:39:16.330
And much more recently, again, if in September
2011 at the time of the casting--that's the

00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:22.910
term, right, the [foreign language 00:39:17],
when Putin and Medvedev announced a job swap--if

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:28.460
somebody said to me or to many of my colleagues
back then that three months from now, there'll

00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:32.740
be a hundred thousand people standing across
the road from the Kremlin demanding Putin's

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:36.620
resignation, you know, we would've thought
that person is living in cloud cuckoo land.

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:37.980
But that is what happened.

00:39:37.980 --> 00:39:45.040
So Russian history has this funny way of showing
dictators and dictatorial regimes and authoritarian

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:51.910
regimes that it's not always up to them what's
gonna happen.

00:39:51.910 --> 00:39:54.320
And this is where I wanna come to the first
part of your question.

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:58.310
You mentioned constitutional changes.

00:39:58.310 --> 00:40:02.570
I think...and you mentioned in terms of what
the regime might want to do, changing the

00:40:02.570 --> 00:40:05.020
system for them to stay in power forever.

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:09.930
But I wanna turn this around a little bit
and talk about the actual importance of having

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:13.090
a constitutional change in Russia.

00:40:13.090 --> 00:40:14.380
This is what we want to do.

00:40:14.380 --> 00:40:19.500
And one of the major policy positions and
positions of principle for our movement, for

00:40:19.500 --> 00:40:25.270
the Open Russia Movement, is to change Russia
from a presidential to a parliamentary republic.

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:30.000
A genuine parliamentary republic, not a fake
one as the one you referred to of a way for

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:31.650
Putin to stay in power forever.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:36.930
But actually a real parliamentary republic
because, you know, when people ask us, "So,

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:41.030
okay you don't want Putin to be in power but
who do you want to replace him with?"

00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:44.560
And we say, "We don't want to replace him
with a who, we'll replace him with a what."

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:47.790
It's not a question of changing the person
at the top, it's a question of changing the

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:48.790
system.

00:40:48.790 --> 00:40:53.910
It hasn't worked out well in Russian history,
I think we can say this certainly by now,

00:40:53.910 --> 00:41:02.040
when one person had all the tools and the...all
instruments of power in his or her hands,

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:03.040
mostly his.

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:07.370
So I think the question is, as Andrei Piontkovsky,
who's in the audience here today once wrote,

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:09.980
"We don't wanna change a bad czar for a good
czar.

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:12.290
We want to have no czar at all in Russia."

00:41:12.290 --> 00:41:14.240
And that is a very important point.

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:16.790
There is no such thing as a good czar.

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:21.080
And so what we wanna to see in Russia after
Vladimir Putin is a much more balanced system.

00:41:21.080 --> 00:41:24.120
Much more parliament...if not a pure parliamentary
system, then at least a much more balanced

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:26.330
mixed presidential-parliamentary system.

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:28.580
Something they have in France, for example.

00:41:28.580 --> 00:41:34.060
We think we should get away from that paradigm
when everything depends on one person.

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:38.250
So the goal of our movement, as well as I
know many other political parties and movements

00:41:38.250 --> 00:41:43.790
in the Russia opposition, is to move to a
different system after Vladimir Putin, in

00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:49.230
one way or another, voluntarily or not very
voluntarily, leaves power.

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:53.470
Because, again, since Russian history shows
us that political change, big political change

00:41:53.470 --> 00:42:00.080
happens quickly, and usually begins unexpectedly,
we need to be preparing for it ahead of time.

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:05.920
Because one of the things that happened, both
in 1991 and in a way in 2011, is at many of

00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:07.950
the democrats were not ready for what was
going to come.

00:42:07.950 --> 00:42:12.000
And we can discuss the many mistakes that
were committed by the democratic government

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:16.001
in the early '90s, and we can discuss the
many mistakes committed by the opposition

00:42:16.001 --> 00:42:19.550
leadership at the height of the protests in
2011-2012.

00:42:19.550 --> 00:42:24.280
I think many of those mistakes were fundamentally
caused by the fact that things happened so

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:25.560
quickly and unexpectedly.

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:30.970
We never know when next big political change
happens in Russia.

00:42:30.970 --> 00:42:32.550
It may be well before 2024.

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:38.770
So I think our task, as a responsible opposition
movement, is to be preparing for that change

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:39.770
now.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:44.060
And this is what we are trying to do because
when things start happening, it's too late

00:42:44.060 --> 00:42:46.770
to sit down and try to figure out what to
do now.

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:49.140
Leon: Well, excellent.

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:53.120
So we have about 15 minutes for questions.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:55.330
So please ask them, you know, in a abbreviated
form.

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:56.330
Please.

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:57.330
Man 1: Hi.

00:42:57.330 --> 00:42:58.330
David Colbin [SP].

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:03.030
Could I just follow up on your last...

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:05.830
Leon: There's a microphone there.

00:43:05.830 --> 00:43:08.630
Man 1: Okay.

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:14.390
By the way, congratulations to the beautiful
new facilities here for AEI, doctor.

00:43:14.390 --> 00:43:21.190
To your point about replacing who and what,
after the coordinating committee period and

00:43:21.190 --> 00:43:26.370
Bolotnaya, it seemed that
the Kremlin had identified Udaltsov as probably

00:43:26.370 --> 00:43:29.010
the most dangerous figure of the opposition.

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:31.800
After all, he was the one they actually jailed.

00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:34.870
And it seemed that the other ones, they slow-rolled.

00:43:34.870 --> 00:43:39.370
Now, putting his personal qualities aside,
one could say demographically, maybe that's

00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:41.860
because of who his base was.

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:50.670
If you think that goal is to replace what
instead of who, what Navalny and Volkov and

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:58.580
the others are doing now and the dignity that
you identified among the youth, what is it

00:43:58.580 --> 00:44:07.760
that you think... the opposition that you'd
like to see is poised outside of Moscow and

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:15.740
Peter to take advantage of this change, as
opposed to an Udaltsov or an Udaltsov-like

00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:20.060
figure speaking to that base, again, outside
of Moscow and Peter?

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:21.950
Vladimir: Okay.

00:44:21.950 --> 00:44:23.520
Thank you for the question.

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:29.610
Well, first of all, let's say that the most
dangerous opposition figure for the Kremlin

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:30.610
was Boris Nemtsov.

00:44:30.610 --> 00:44:33.730
They did something much worse to him that
they did to Udaltsov.

00:44:33.730 --> 00:44:37.970
Udaltsov, thankfully, is out of prison now,
a couple of months.

00:44:37.970 --> 00:44:44.430
He was in prison, if you count home arrest,
for more than four years.

00:44:44.430 --> 00:44:49.470
And just as a footnote, we have more than
a hundred political prisoners in Russia today,

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:50.470
still.

00:44:50.470 --> 00:44:51.840
Some of the prominent ones were released.

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:54.560
Udaltsov was released,
Darya Polyudova was released.

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:57.190
The remaining Bolotnaya prisoners were released, I think all of them

00:44:57.190 --> 00:44:58.190
now.

00:44:58.190 --> 00:44:59.190
Literally in the last few months.

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:02.770
But let's not forget there are more than a
hundred political prisoners in Russia.

00:45:02.770 --> 00:45:07.890
Sorry, 117 by the latest count, by the Memorial
Human Rights Center, itself, of course now

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:12.560
designated as a foreign agent, the Human Rights
Organizations once founded by Andrei Sakharov.

00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:18.050
And they use very conservative, and even I'll
say restrictive criteria to designate somebody

00:45:18.050 --> 00:45:20.050
as a political prisoner.

00:45:20.050 --> 00:45:24.850
And even by that criteria, it's a criteria
based on resolution 1900 of the parliamentary

00:45:24.850 --> 00:45:26.490
assembly of the Council of Europe.

00:45:26.490 --> 00:45:32.000
And even according to those criteria, we have
117 political prisoners in Russia.

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:40.240
Just to put it in context, in 1975 once Sakharov
wrote his Nobel Prize lecture, he listed 126

00:45:40.240 --> 00:45:41.240
prisoners of conscience.

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:44.640
And that was of course USSR, which was twice
as big as Russia.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:48.420
So we're back to the same kind of figures.

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:55.950
I think...the most important point, I think,
that you refer to is the need to go outside

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:57.070
Moscow and Saint Petersburg.

00:45:57.070 --> 00:46:01.590
Those has been...well, I think for centuries,
you could say the traditional hotbeds of political

00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:02.590
activity.

00:46:02.590 --> 00:46:04.970
Everything in Russia always happened in Moscow
and Saint Petersburg.

00:46:04.970 --> 00:46:12.550
And with the big protests that we saw in these
last few months, we saw this paradigm at least

00:46:12.550 --> 00:46:13.950
beginning to be broken.

00:46:13.950 --> 00:46:18.240
Especially with the June wave, when almost
200,000 cities, literally from Leningrad to

00:46:18.240 --> 00:46:20.540
Vladivostok, took part of these protests.

00:46:20.540 --> 00:46:25.120
And some of these places have not seen any
political protest...well at least since '91,

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:26.780
maybe since 1917, some of them.

00:46:26.780 --> 00:46:32.470
And now as we see Navalny, for example, go
around the country in his unofficial presidential

00:46:32.470 --> 00:46:36.740
campaign which he's conducting, he's gathering,
you know, thousands of people in the squares

00:46:36.740 --> 00:46:39.450
and places where nobody thought any political
activity could exist.

00:46:39.450 --> 00:46:43.790
[foreign language 00:46:39], I mean, people...
not the places you automatically think of

00:46:43.790 --> 00:46:46.840
when you think about opposition activity in
Russia.

00:46:46.840 --> 00:46:47.990
So I agree completely with your point.

00:46:47.990 --> 00:46:53.790
I think it's very important to go beyond Moscow
and Saint Petersburg and to go outside...to

00:46:53.790 --> 00:46:56.290
go to the country, to different parts of the
country.

00:46:56.290 --> 00:46:57.670
We try to do this as Open Russia as well.

00:46:57.670 --> 00:47:01.710
We now have 28 regional branches, as of this
week.

00:47:01.710 --> 00:47:04.490
The last one was opened three days ago in
[foreign language 00:47:03].

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:08.580
And we feel it's important also to break this
paradigm of everything happening just in Moscow

00:47:08.580 --> 00:47:09.580
and Saint Petersburg.

00:47:09.580 --> 00:47:14.530
And, you know, in the last three years since
we've relaunched Open Russia, I've seen more

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:16.650
of Russia than in my whole previous life.

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:21.960
I mean, because we try to spread our work
and do a lot of work in the regions.

00:47:21.960 --> 00:47:31.210
Udaltsov, of course, is somebody who represents
a very much left wing political tradition.

00:47:31.210 --> 00:47:33.710
And of course, since he was jailed, he's also
just like Navalny.

00:47:33.710 --> 00:47:35.570
He's not allowed to contest in the elections
now.

00:47:35.570 --> 00:47:38.660
They just...they made up this law a few years
ago, which by the way, there's nothing in

00:47:38.660 --> 00:47:43.130
the Russian constitution about any such limitations
on taking part in elections.

00:47:43.130 --> 00:47:47.670
The only limitations the Russian constitution
mentions is that if you're actually in prison,

00:47:47.670 --> 00:47:50.330
according to a court sentence, then you cannot
take part in elections.

00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:55.370
Or if you are insane or if you are below the
age of 35 if you're talking about presidential

00:47:55.370 --> 00:47:56.370
elections.

00:47:56.370 --> 00:47:57.370
No other limitations.

00:47:57.370 --> 00:48:02.170
But Putin and his matte printer, the state
Duma, have rubber stamped all these additional

00:48:02.170 --> 00:48:03.620
restrictions in the last few years now.

00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:06.220
If you have dual citizenship, you cannot run
in elections.

00:48:06.220 --> 00:48:08.900
Even if you have residency permanently abroad,
you cannot run in elections.

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:13.360
If you have even a suspended court sentence,
which is what Navalny have, you cannot run.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:17.100
Even if you are released for prison, like
Udaltsov, like Khodorkovsky, for example,

00:48:17.100 --> 00:48:18.330
you cannot run any kind of election.

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:21.120
So they put up all these additional barriers.

00:48:21.120 --> 00:48:26.700
We think, of course, needless to say, that,
you know, every Russian citizen who's eligible

00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:30.960
according to the constitution, including Udaltsov,
should have the right to run in elections.

00:48:30.960 --> 00:48:33.880
And we also think that it's important that
the opposition--and this is go back to the

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:40.230
what and not the who--that the Russian opposition
has many strong political figures within it

00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:42.190
of different political persuasion.

00:48:42.190 --> 00:48:44.740
People like Udaltsov on the left, people like
Navalny.

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:48.110
People like Dmitry Gudkov, who
was already mentioned, the former member of

00:48:48.110 --> 00:48:49.110
state Duma.

00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:52.000
People like Lef Schlossberg, a very prominent
figure in the Russian opposition, one of the

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:53.360
leaders of the Yabloko Party.

00:48:53.360 --> 00:48:59.900
He was the person who, three years ago now,
provided the first documented, definitive

00:48:59.900 --> 00:49:03.280
proof of the presence of regular Russian troops
in Ukraine.

00:49:03.280 --> 00:49:05.131
Which was, of course, something the Kremlin
lied about and denied.

00:49:05.131 --> 00:49:10.310
And when he did this--this was in Pskov, he's
a member of the regional parliament in Pskov--and

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:13.320
it was a Pskov paratrooper division that was
sent into Ukraine.

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:18.850
Members of it were killed there and then they
were brought back in body bags and buried

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:23.240
like dogs in unmarked graves with numbers
instead of names because the Russian defense

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:25.090
ministry did not want to admit that they were
there.

00:49:25.090 --> 00:49:28.240
And Schlossberg found them, he took pictures
of them, and he published it in his newspaper.

00:49:28.240 --> 00:49:32.840
He was beaten up, he was in a coma in hospital,
he was stripped off his parliamentary seat.

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:38.120
And then last year, he was elected back to
the regional parliament by the people of Pskov.

00:49:38.120 --> 00:49:39.980
He's a member of parliament now again.

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:44.330
People like him, people like Yevgeny Roizman,
who is the current mayor of Yekaterinburg,

00:49:44.330 --> 00:49:47.691
the fourth largest city in Russia, one of
the most prominent figures in the opposition

00:49:47.691 --> 00:49:48.691
today.

00:49:48.691 --> 00:49:51.060
He wanted to run for regional governor from
Yabloko earlier this year.

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:53.500
He was again blocked from the ballot, not
allowed to run.

00:49:53.500 --> 00:49:58.680
So we think there should be many strong political
figures in the Russian opposition.

00:49:58.680 --> 00:50:04.490
And although I disagree with probably most
of what Sergei Udaltsov stands for politically.

00:50:04.490 --> 00:50:08.490
I certainly think that he should have an absolute
right to run in elections.

00:50:08.490 --> 00:50:13.380
And I think when we have a freely elected
parliament in Russia one day, he'll most likely

00:50:13.380 --> 00:50:14.380
to be a member of it

00:50:14.380 --> 00:50:15.380
Leon: Anders.

00:50:15.380 --> 00:50:19.510
Anders: Anders Aslund, Atlantic
Council.

00:50:19.510 --> 00:50:21.600
Thank you very much for an excellent analysis.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:25.720
And as always, I have a standard question.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:27.400
What can we do?

00:50:27.400 --> 00:50:30.020
And I'm thinking particular of two issues.

00:50:30.020 --> 00:50:40.250
One is this sanction law now, the section
241 of pointing out people closer to the Kremlin.

00:50:40.250 --> 00:50:42.380
Do you think that this can be effective?

00:50:42.380 --> 00:50:48.370
And the other question is Putin got obviously
furious over the release of the Panama Papers

00:50:48.370 --> 00:50:55.370
in April 2016 and all that it exposed about
him.

00:50:55.370 --> 00:51:02.800
Do you think it would be effective if the
U.S. really reveals the riches of the Putin

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:06.400
crowd that obviously exist in this country?

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:07.400
Vladimir: Thank you, Anders.

00:51:07.400 --> 00:51:12.170
And it's always great to see you, and thank
you for the questions.

00:51:12.170 --> 00:51:17.370
When we were discussing this issue, Boris
Nemtsov came up with this formula back a few

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:18.910
years ago.

00:51:18.910 --> 00:51:23.060
When people ask him, what should the west
do with regard to what's happening in Russia?

00:51:23.060 --> 00:51:27.650
He would always say, "Don't punish the country.

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:31.760
Go after the scroundels." [foreign language
00:51:30].

00:51:31.760 --> 00:51:35.140
And this is, of course, what section 241 does.

00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:39.180
And you had an excellent panel up on Capitol
Hill a couple of weeks ago, along was Andre

00:51:39.180 --> 00:51:41.320
and other colleagues.

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:47.940
And I think this is...in this whole law that
was passed last summer, this probably the

00:51:47.940 --> 00:51:50.790
most important provision.

00:51:50.790 --> 00:51:55.850
Section 241 is a provision that tasks the
department of the Treasury, the State department

00:51:55.850 --> 00:52:00.910
in coordination with the Director of National
Intelligence, to publish a report by the deadline

00:52:00.910 --> 00:52:09.370
of February 2nd of next year, 2018, detailing
the people and naming them by names, the people

00:52:09.370 --> 00:52:13.750
who have political proximity to Putin and
his regime, the oligarchs, the politically

00:52:13.750 --> 00:52:19.980
powerful officials, to detail their involvement
in corruption, to detail the non-Russian business

00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:23.360
associations, so the foreign business association
they have.

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:30.270
And I know for a fact that in the last few
weeks, many of these people have sent emissaries

00:52:30.270 --> 00:52:35.000
to this city, to try to approach, you know,
lobbyists and people on the hill to see if

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:39.840
it will be possible for them not to be named
in this report.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:45.050
And I think, yet again, the Kremlin shows
what is the most sensitive point for them.

00:52:45.050 --> 00:52:54.300
And the most sensitive point for them is when
you go after their personal financial interests.

00:52:54.300 --> 00:52:56.410
And this is what we always say.

00:52:56.410 --> 00:52:59.530
Again, as Boris said, "Don't punish the country,
go after the scroundels."

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:06.150
We do not think it is right to punish an entire
country for the actions of a small, unelected

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:08.760
clique sitting in the Kremlin.

00:53:08.760 --> 00:53:16.420
And this is why the Magnitsky Act was so brilliant
in the principle that it introduced, that

00:53:16.420 --> 00:53:20.490
you should actually assign responsibility
for corruption and for human rights abuse

00:53:20.490 --> 00:53:23.780
where is it due, to the people who perpetrated
it.

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:27.700
And so I would say this would be the most
important thing that western countries can

00:53:27.700 --> 00:53:28.700
do.

00:53:28.700 --> 00:53:34.000
And if you listen to Kremlin propaganda, they
will tell you that, you know, we members of

00:53:34.000 --> 00:53:40.040
the Russian opposition go to the west and
we ask for money and for political support.

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:44.020
Or we ask you to effect regime change in Russia,
whatever other nonsense they come up with.

00:53:44.020 --> 00:53:46.260
Of course, none of that is true.

00:53:46.260 --> 00:53:48.350
We're not asking for support for us.

00:53:48.350 --> 00:53:54.480
The only thing we ask our friends and counterparts
in western democracies is that you stop, in

00:53:54.480 --> 00:53:57.060
effect, supporting the Putin regime.

00:53:57.060 --> 00:54:01.210
First of all, by treating him as respectable
and worthy partner on the international stage,

00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:04.060
which amazingly, after everything that's happened,
some western leaders are still are trying

00:54:04.060 --> 00:54:05.060
to do.

00:54:05.060 --> 00:54:10.890
But also, and perhaps more importantly, stop
supporting the Putin regime by allowing his

00:54:10.890 --> 00:54:16.240
cronies to use western countries as havens
for their looted wealth.

00:54:16.240 --> 00:54:20.850
You know, as havens to store the money they
have stolen from the Russian people.

00:54:20.850 --> 00:54:23.900
Because again, those people want to steal
in Russia but spend in the west.

00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:27.331
And by allowing them and their dirty money
into the western countries and the western

00:54:27.331 --> 00:54:32.350
financial systems, western democracies have,
in effect, been enabling--I have no other

00:54:32.350 --> 00:54:35.840
word--enabling this corruption and this human
rights abuse.

00:54:35.840 --> 00:54:39.900
And so it's very heartening to see that four
countries already, the U.S., Canada, Estonia,

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:44.970
and Lithuania have passed full blown Magnitsky
Acts that introduce this principle that if

00:54:44.970 --> 00:54:48.850
you engage in corruption and human right abuse,
you will no longer be entitled to a visa and

00:54:48.850 --> 00:54:51.860
to using the financial and banking system
of western countries.

00:54:51.860 --> 00:54:53.520
We hope more countries do that.

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:58.990
And this section 241 is, of course, very,
very important.

00:54:58.990 --> 00:55:03.700
And although it does not entail sanctions,
per se--it's just report, it's just publication--I

00:55:03.700 --> 00:55:08.080
think given their nervous reaction, it's a
safe bet to say that those types of things

00:55:08.080 --> 00:55:11.150
are the most effective ones.

00:55:11.150 --> 00:55:15.730
And I'd also mention another thing that I
think is important.

00:55:15.730 --> 00:55:21.150
And I know some people dismiss this as small
and symbolic, what I'm gonna say now.

00:55:21.150 --> 00:55:24.870
And although it is symbolic, it's certainly
not small, Russia is a country of symbols,

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:25.870
as you know.

00:55:25.870 --> 00:55:29.300
I always remind people of this, when after
the victory in...over the coup leaders in

00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:34.020
August 1991, Moscovites went to the Lubyanka
Square to take down the statue of Dzerzhinsky

00:55:34.020 --> 00:55:35.670
because that was a symbol.

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:40.040
One of the first things that Putin did when
he came to power was to return the Soviet-era

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:41.630
Stalinist national anthem.

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:45.780
Again, a symbol of what was to come and that
was a very powerful message for those who

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:46.780
were willing to listen.

00:55:46.780 --> 00:55:50.560
So what I'm gonna say now, that's a symbol
but that's a very important one.

00:55:50.560 --> 00:55:55.170
Tomorrow, on December the 6th--and this is
actually the reason I'm in Washington this

00:55:55.170 --> 00:56:02.520
week--there will be a hearing at the Washington
D.C. city council on the bill that would designate

00:56:02.520 --> 00:56:07.920
the block in front of the Russian Embassy
here in Washington on Wisconsin Avenue Northwest,

00:56:07.920 --> 00:56:12.550
as Boris Nemtsov plaza, in memory and in honor
of the late Russian opposition leader, Boris

00:56:12.550 --> 00:56:13.550
Nemtsov.

00:56:13.550 --> 00:56:18.990
We tried to move this initiative initially
through congress, where it was blocked by

00:56:18.990 --> 00:56:22.280
Senator Bob Corker, chairman of the foreign
relations committee as was reported last week

00:56:22.280 --> 00:56:24.490
in the "Washington Post".

00:56:24.490 --> 00:56:27.280
And as Alexei Venediktov, the editor
in chief of Ekho Moskvy radio

00:56:27.280 --> 00:56:33.800
station said publicly this week, he told the
story of how in July, during the G20 summit,

00:56:33.800 --> 00:56:37.660
there was a meeting, that was the first meeting
between Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin.

00:56:37.660 --> 00:56:41.350
But before it, there was a meeting between
Sergei Lavrov, the Russian foreign minister,

00:56:41.350 --> 00:56:43.720
and Rex Tillerson, the U.S. secretary of state.

00:56:43.720 --> 00:56:47.140
And apparently, this is according to what
Benedictov said, three days ago literally,

00:56:47.140 --> 00:56:51.840
apparently at that meeting in July, they recited...they
went through different issues that need to

00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:56.100
be addressed and resolved for the bilateral
relationship to get, quote, back on track.

00:56:56.100 --> 00:56:59.660
They talked about Crimea and sanctions and
Ukraine and Syria.

00:56:59.660 --> 00:57:06.010
And apparently according to Benedictov, foreign
minister Sergei Lavrov specifically asked

00:57:06.010 --> 00:57:12.040
Tillerson not to rename that block in front
of the Russian Embassy after Boris Nemtsov.

00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:14.830
So again, anybody who tells you that symbolic
things are not important, I think that's the

00:57:14.830 --> 00:57:15.890
best answer.

00:57:15.890 --> 00:57:20.560
So from what we understand, Mr. Corker, because
of this request from the administration, blocked

00:57:20.560 --> 00:57:22.280
it.

00:57:22.280 --> 00:57:26.530
But thankfully there is also jurisdiction
of the D.C. city council over D.C., not just

00:57:26.530 --> 00:57:27.530
congress.

00:57:27.530 --> 00:57:30.750
So the D.C. city council has taken up this
initiative, and they're moving ahead with

00:57:30.750 --> 00:57:31.750
it.

00:57:31.750 --> 00:57:34.360
Tomorrow is gonna be the first and the only
public hearing on the bill.

00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:39.500
I will be testifying in favor of this measure,
as will Zhanna Nemtsova, Boris Nemtsov's daughter,

00:57:39.500 --> 00:57:41.460
who is flying in today.

00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:43.600
She'll be testifying tomorrow.

00:57:43.600 --> 00:57:48.840
And the reason this is so important for us,
what I'm gonna say at that hearing tomorrow,

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:55.370
it's seems we're not allowed to honor the
memory of a Russian statesman in Russia.

00:57:55.370 --> 00:58:00.850
The astonishing thing is that the Kremlin
and the Putin regime are still fighting Boris

00:58:00.850 --> 00:58:03.370
Nemtsov today even after he's dead.

00:58:03.370 --> 00:58:08.870
They have rejected every initiative, every
public petition for any kind of commemoration

00:58:08.870 --> 00:58:09.870
in Moscow.

00:58:09.870 --> 00:58:12.660
Forget about a street name, not even a small
sign, nothing.

00:58:12.660 --> 00:58:15.930
And you know the reason they gave for this,
they said there's a lack of consensus.

00:58:15.930 --> 00:58:20.240
Now we have streets in Moscow named after
Hugo Chavez, the late Valenzuela dictator.

00:58:20.240 --> 00:58:25.280
We have a street in Moscow, in the south of
Moscow, in Butovo, named

00:58:25.280 --> 00:58:27.040
after Akhmad Kadyrov, the former Chechen strongman.

00:58:27.040 --> 00:58:31.670
This is the guy who once declared a Jihad
against Russia, and once called on his followers

00:58:31.670 --> 00:58:34.350
to, quote, kill as many Russians as possible,
end of quote.

00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:37.980
There was no problem with consensus for naming
a street in Moscow after that guy.

00:58:37.980 --> 00:58:41.830
But the former deputy prime minister of the
Russian federation, there seems to be a big

00:58:41.830 --> 00:58:42.830
problem with it.

00:58:42.830 --> 00:58:45.810
And there were signs that were put up by the
residents of the apartment building where

00:58:45.810 --> 00:58:47.320
Boris lived in Moscow, in Yuroslavl.

00:58:47.320 --> 00:58:50.280
They were taken down within days.

00:58:50.280 --> 00:58:54.430
More than that, almost every night, the Moscow
municipal authorities and the Moscow police

00:58:54.430 --> 00:58:59.840
come in to the bridge to the unofficial memorial
on the spot where he was killed--because more

00:58:59.840 --> 00:59:05.460
than 2 1/2 years on, people still bring flowers
and light candles and put pictures, kind of

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:10.210
an unofficial makeshift memorial--and they
take those flowers away and they pillage it

00:59:10.210 --> 00:59:11.210
and they destroy it.

00:59:11.210 --> 00:59:12.210
And you should see the videos.

00:59:12.210 --> 00:59:15.250
There are videos of this on YouTube and Facebook
filmed by the volunteers.

00:59:15.250 --> 00:59:22.110
You have grown man, in police uniforms, stealing
flowers in the middle of the night.

00:59:22.110 --> 00:59:25.270
So they're still fighting him, they're still
fighting his memory.

00:59:25.270 --> 00:59:29.380
And so what we're gonna say tomorrow at that
council hearing is that apparently we're not

00:59:29.380 --> 00:59:31.610
allowed to honor a Russian statesman in Russia.

00:59:31.610 --> 00:59:35.350
So we're very grateful to citizens and to
elective representatives of free countries

00:59:35.350 --> 00:59:39.340
who are stepping in to do what we cannot do.

00:59:39.340 --> 00:59:43.300
And there was, of course, a precedent for
this, also set in this town and also connected

00:59:43.300 --> 00:59:44.300
with Russia.

00:59:44.300 --> 00:59:49.690
In 1984, the U.S. congress designated a small
stretch of 16th Street Northwest, in front

00:59:49.690 --> 00:59:53.790
of the old Soviet embassy, it's now the Russian
Ambassador's residence, in honor of Andrei

00:59:53.790 --> 00:59:56.130
Sakharov, who was then in internal exile in
Gorky.

00:59:56.130 --> 00:59:58.410
This was 1984.

00:59:58.410 --> 01:00:03.690
And of course, as you can imagine, the Politbureau
and the soviet foreign ministry were furious

01:00:03.690 --> 01:00:04.900
when this happened.

01:00:04.900 --> 01:00:09.390
But I always remind people, only seven years
after that--and this goes back to the question

01:00:09.390 --> 01:00:13.620
you're saying, you know, when can we expect
change in Russia--only seven years after 1984,

01:00:13.620 --> 01:00:20.540
in 1991, the Russian Ambassador installed,
proudly installed a bust of Andrei Sakaharov

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:23.560
inside the embassy itself.

01:00:23.560 --> 01:00:25.160
And it was still, by the way, standing there
the last time I was there.

01:00:25.160 --> 01:00:27.310
I was declared non-grata in Russian embassy
in 2012.

01:00:27.310 --> 01:00:29.390
So the last time I was been there, that was
five years ago, but it was still standing

01:00:29.390 --> 01:00:30.680
there.

01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:32.290
And this is how things change in Russia.

01:00:32.290 --> 01:00:38.650
So I have absolutely no doubt that there will
come a day when the Russian state is proud

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:43.240
to have its embassy Washington standing on
Boris Nemtsov Plaza.

01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:47.960
And I know that they will all be very grateful
for our friends in western democracies, including

01:00:47.960 --> 01:00:51.020
here in the U.S. who made this statement and
made this point.

01:00:51.020 --> 01:00:53.990
It's very symbolic but it's certainly not
small.

01:00:53.990 --> 01:00:55.400
Leon: Well.

01:00:55.400 --> 01:00:58.260
I'm afraid we don't have time for any more
questions.

